WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.860
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.195 --> 00:00:07.320
JEN PERSON: Give me one
minute, and I will show you

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.840
how persistent disks are a
great block storage option

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.340
for your virtual machine.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.830
Persistent disk is high
performance solid state

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.810
and hard disk
drive block storage

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:17.520
that can be attached
to instances

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:21.750
running in Compute Engine
or Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:23.930
Standard persistent
disk, or HDD,

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:26.370
is low cost and best
suited for large data

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:30.300
processing workloads that
primarily use sequential I/Os.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:33.660
SSDs are better for high
performance apps and databases

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:37.200
that require lower
latency and more IOPS.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.330
You can create persistent
disks through the console

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:43.020
by selecting the disk type
and where you want it.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.540
Then attach it to
the virtual machine.

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:48.030
You could also use the G
Cloud command line interface

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:49.950
to create and attach the disk.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.440
Persistent disks are
located independently

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:54.960
from VM instances,
meaning you can detach

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.510
remove disks to keep
your data even after you

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.520
delete your instances.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:03.000
Sharing content is also a snap
since many virtual machines can

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.800
read data from a single disk.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.050
And you don't have to worry
about downtime because you

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:10.080
can resize the storage on
demand without having to restart

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.810
the VM or reattach the volumes.

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:15.780
For high availability, create
regional storage replicating

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.510
and automated
geo-replicated snapshots.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:20.670
On the security front,
persistent disks

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:22.980
are automatically encrypted
to protect your data.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:25.650
So next time you're looking
to add reliable and affordable

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:27.570
storage to your
VM, take a minute

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:29.220
and look at persistent disk.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:33.920
To learn more, check out
cloud.google.com/persistentdisk.

